OCC - Optimistic Concurrency Control => Optimistic Locking

Strategic trade off between throughput and data integrity

Look before you leap

Assumes low contention (that most transactions will not interfere with each other)

no locking (locking is pessimistic)

system allows transaction to proceed & only checks the conflicts at the very end.

3 phase life cycle : 
1. Read/execution phase
2. Validation phase
3. Commit/Write phase

Implementation strategies:
1. Version number
2. Timestamp (last_updated_at)
3. Checksum/hash/fingerprinting (check hash of entire record)

Key trade offs:

Pros : 
1. high concurrency: No "stop the world", excellent for read heavy workloads
2. deadlock prevention: no locks
3. Scalability: better for stateless architecture

Cons:
1. Contention Penalty: if many users fight for same resource(eg, flash sale), the high abort/retry rate waste more CPU and IO, giving worse performance than pessimistic locking.

2. the "ABA" problem: if value changes from A to B and back to A, system won't detect (version number can)

3. Starvation: a larger slow transaction might keep getting abort by smaller fast transactions.

When to choose:
Choose OCC when cost of retry is lower than cost of waiting (eg, collab tools, google docs, git, inventory management, etc.)
choose pessimistic locking, when contention is high & latency of a retry is not affordable.(banking, ledger, etc.)

